@cc ===========================================================================
@cc CHATCC:MODE - build the PLANC chat client on D100.
@cc
@cc   @MODE CHATCC:MODE,,
@cc
@cc The name is short on purpose. Our file transfer opens a file with a QFORM
@cc string of at most 15 bytes holding the specification, an apostrophe and the
@cc access letter - so 13 characters of name - and a CREATE carries its own
@cc quotes inside that budget. "CHAT-CC:MODE" is 14 with the quotes and is
@cc refused before a byte goes out; "CHATCC:MODE" is 13 and fits.
@cc
@cc Needs these under SYSTEM:
@cc
@cc   CHAT:PLNC            the client, carried over by our own file transfer
@cc   XMP-B02:DEFS         the constants          )  the real COSMOS binding,
@cc   XMP-B02:IMPT         the declarations       )  product ND-10609, put on
@cc   XMP-100-1-B02:BRF    the code               )  by COS-LIB-IN-B02:PROG
@cc
@cc Our own XMPIMP:INCL and the (UTILITY)XMSG-PL-VALUES-L:INCL copy are NO
@cc longer used by this build. XMPIMP was written from the manual to stand in
@cc for the missing XMP:IMPT; the real file is here now and is the better
@cc source. Do not include XMSG-PL-VALUES-L alongside XMP-B02:DEFS - they share
@cc 184 constant names.
@cc
@cc THREE THINGS THIS BUILD GETS RIGHT AND A HAND-TYPED ONE USUALLY DOES NOT:
@cc
@cc 1. THE SOURCE MUST BE CRLF. A file carried over with bare LF makes the
@cc    compiler answer "LINE IS TOO LONG" on EVERY line, including a line one
@cc    character long - it never finds an end-of-line and fills its buffer. The
@cc    files on the machine are the proof: XMSG-PL-VALUES-L:INCL came off D100
@cc    with 383 CR and 383 LF, and no byte with bit 7 set. Text files here are
@cc    plain 7-bit ASCII with CRLF; the even-parity rule is for terminal INPUT,
@cc    not for files.
@cc
@cc 2. EVERY INCLUDED FILE MUST END WITH $EOF. Without it the compiler runs off
@cc    the end of the include and takes that as the end of the WHOLE compile -
@cc    and reports 0 DIAGNOSTICS while doing it. That reads as a clean build
@cc    that quietly skipped the entire program. The tell is the line count: it
@cc    stops at exactly the line of the $INCLUDE, and the next command answers
@cc    COMMAND NOT PERMITTED WITHIN MODULES because the module never closed.
@cc
@cc 3. THE XMP LIBRARY IS LINKED IN A SEPARATE STEP, BY BRF-LINKER-C01.
@cc    Nothing pulls it in on its own, so every XMPF... call stays unresolved
@cc    and the build ends with "- LOADER MESSAGE: UNDEFINED ENTRIES".
@cc
@cc    AND IT MUST BE XMP-100-1-B02, NOT XMSG-LIBRARY-L03. The latter was
@cc    tried first and holds no XMPF code at all - asked with
@cc    LIST-BRF-ENTRIES it answers eleven internal entries (XBINI, XQVER,
@cc    XMRER, XMERR and friends) and loading it moves the free address by
@cc    exactly nothing. It is the NPL-level support library. The XMPF code
@cc    comes only from the ND-10609 product.
@cc
@cc    Both ways of doing it inside the compiler were tried on D100 and both
@cc    fail, which is why this build has two steps:
@cc
@cc      LOAD before COMPILE is ACCEPTED AND DOES NOTHING. A library supplies
@cc      only the units something already refers to, and at that point nothing
@cc      refers to anything. It prints no message at all - the worst kind of
@cc      wrong, because the build looks the same as a good one.
@cc
@cc      LOAD after COMPILE is REFUSED with COMMAND NOT PERMITTED WITHIN
@cc      MODULES. Ending the source with $EOF does not rescue it: $EOF in the
@cc      MAIN source ends the compiler SESSION, so the LOAD is handed to
@cc      SINTRAN instead, which answers ILLEGAL COMMAND and aborts the job.
@cc
@cc    So: PROG-FILE is NOT set, the compiler is asked only for a BRF, and
@cc    BRF-LINKER-C01 puts the three pieces together. That also means the BRF
@cc    is a real object file - with PROG-FILE set the compiler links straight
@cc    into the program file and leaves CHAT:BRF empty, which loads as nothing
@cc    and reports FREE: P 000000-177777.
@cc
@cc    Order inside the linker matters the other way round: the program first,
@cc    THEN the libraries that satisfy it.
@cc ===========================================================================

@cc --- clear the old build; a "NO SUCH FILE NAME" on a first run is expected --
@DELETE-FILE CHAT:PROG
@DELETE-FILE CHAT:LIST
@DELETE-FILE CHAT:BRF

@cc --- step 1: compile to an object file ------------------------------------
@cc Quoting: no quotes means the file must already exist, "quotes" means create.
@cc No PROG-FILE here on purpose - see note 3.
@PLANC-100-F00
COMPILE CHAT:PLNC,"CHAT:LIST","CHAT"
EXIT

@cc --- step 2: link the program, the XMSG library and the PLANC runtime -----
@cc 5MON_P comes from PLANC-1BANK-F00 - it is what MONITOR_CALL compiles into,
@cc so the runtime is needed even though this program calls nothing else in it.
@BRF-LINKER-C01
PROGRAM-FILE "CHAT"
LOAD CHAT
LIBRARY-MODE ON
LOAD XMP-100-1-B02
LOAD PLANC-1BANK-F00
LIST-ENTRIES-UNDEFINED
EXIT

@cc ===========================================================================
@cc CHECK THE LISTING BEFORE BELIEVING THE BUILD.
@cc
@cc   @LIST-FILE CHAT:LIST,,       and look for "*** ERROR"
@cc
@cc The compiler prints its diagnostics as it goes, and on a long source they
@cc scroll off the top of a 24-line screen long before the summary appears - so
@cc "0 DIAGNOSTICS" at the bottom of the SECOND pass can sit directly under a
@cc compile that had three. The count that matters is the one on the COMPILE
@cc line, not the one after EXIT, and the listing has both plus the offending
@cc lines. Not one "*** ERROR" in CHAT:LIST is the only green light here.
@cc ===========================================================================
@cc Built CHAT:PROG. Run it with @CHAT
@cc
@cc If the count of LINES COMPILED is far short of the source, look for the
@cc missing $EOF before looking at the program.
@cc ===========================================================================
